{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2927","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2927","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2927\",\"parent_revision\":\"bc77293c3caf257cab60462f7f36cbe1f76f8678\",\"root_revision\":\"83855873f3c634bcdab811f0932d8730d8f1b4142a7bab5739605bf0f62c311a\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P2\\ntype: Chore\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-17\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nWorktree-isolated agents dispatched via the Agent tool (particularly the python-engineering:python-cli-architect agent type) sometimes write to this repo's git-tracked `.claude/agent-memory/<agent>/` directory mid-task, as part of their normal operating behavior, when they discover something durable worth recording. Delegation prompts written for these agents specify a \\\"Definition of Success\\\" and commit instructions scoped only to the task's actual code/doc deliverable (e.g. \\\"commit these specific files, push, open a PR\\\") \u2014 they do not say what to do with any `.claude/agent-memory/` files the agent also wrote.\\n\\nObserved twice in one session: an agent correctly avoids bundling the unrelated memory file into its feature-branch commit (good instinct \u2014 keeps the PR diff scoped), but then has no instruction for what to do with the memory file instead, so it is left uncommitted in the agent's worktree. When the worktree is later torn down (after the feature branch merges and the orchestrator runs `git worktree remove`), the uncommitted memory file is only caught if the orchestrator happens to run `git status` first \u2014 otherwise it is silently lost. Both observed instances required the orchestrator to notice the uncommitted file, recover its content, and open a separate small docs PR to land it.\\n\\nWhere observed: this session, twice \u2014 once with a PR #2918 fix-agent's uncommitted memory file (recovered and merged via a separate PR), and once with a PR #2924 extraction-agent's uncommitted memory file (`.claude/agent-memory/python-engineering-python-cli-architect/project_worktree_shared_object_db_no_dash_c_needed.md`, recovered and merged via PR #2926).\\n\\nImpact: agent-authored operational knowledge is at risk of silent loss whenever a worktree-isolated agent both (a) writes to `.claude/agent-memory/` and (b) is not the orchestrator that later inspects the worktree before removal. This is a repeated failure shape, not a one-off \u2014 the underlying gap is structural (delegation prompts + worktree cleanup process), not a one-time agent mistake.\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **maintainer of the project infrastructure**, I want to **worktree-isolated agent delegation prompts don't specify handling for .claude/agent-memory/ writes separate from the task's main deliverable** so that **the project infrastructure stays healthy**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation \u2014 recurring pattern, flagged directly by the user after the second occurrence\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-17\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"e71cfb01849d0bf1b2d88375ee994aec18dccaf02b43f2c0e7b62696a094bfd5\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABPUYDUg\"}"}